	title = {“Casual women, Silent Titans”: a decolonial feminist interpretation of Ghanaian female soldiers in Silent Titans},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {African Identities},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1080/14725843.2025.2541683},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105012860747&doi=10.1080%2F14725843.2025.2541683&partnerID=40&md5=7156a60f254afe1dcda8b438446d508a},
	affiliations = {Kwame Nkrumah University of Science & Technology, Department of Communication Design, Kumasi, Ghana},
	abstract = {This paper examines how the Ghanaian documentary Silent Titans, directed by Magdalene Kwashie, highlights the gender dynamics and lived experiences of women within the Ghana Armed Forces, a historically male-dominated institution. Through a qualitative film analysis grounded in decolonial feminist theory, the study explores not only the women’s testimonies but also the film’s elements, including mise-en-scene, performance, cinematography, sound and editing, to understand how cinematic language constructs gendered meaning. Decolonial feminist theory provides a critical lens for analysing how the documentary challenges colonial and patriarchal frameworks that have historically marginalised African women’s narratives. The film positions the participants not as passive observers but as embodied agents who speak, perform, and adapt within constrained structures. The analysis reveals the affective and political tensions these women traverse, including balancing career and family life, confronting gendered hierarchies, and asserting voice within the rigid protocols of military discipline. Silent Titans emerges as a site of visual resistance, offering a counter-narrative that affirms African women’s agency, complexity, and historical presence. This study contributes to African feminist film research by demonstrating documentary films can be utilised to understand and challenge cultural issues, thereby highlighting the complex struggles with identity, power, and representation in postcolonial contexts. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {African documentary film; decolonial feminist theory; documentary film; gender and military; male-dominated professions; visual storytelling},
